      {
        "id": "DSD_SWAC_PV",
        "version": "1.0",
        "agencyID": "OECD.SWAC",
        "isFinal": true,
        "name": "Trends of political violence in West Africa",
        "description": "The weekly rates of political violent events (and of fatalities related to those events) represents the expected number of events per week (or fatalities per week) after smoothing out weekly fluctuations. The weekly rates are calculated at the national level, i.e. including all political violent events within the country under analysis; or at the regional level (a group of neighbour countries or parts of those countries); or violent groups, i.e., including all the events in which the group participates.\nFor event based indicators, the weekly rate is the average number of events per week.\nFor fatalities based indicators, the weekly rate is the average number of fatalities per week from those events.\nThere are three types of events: Violence against civilians, Battles, Explosions and remote violence. \nThere is one data point (weekly rate) for every week since January 1st, 2015, for every type of event and for the sum of all three event\u2019s types. Weekly rates are calculated for the events, and for the fatalities related to those events.\nThe calculation of the weekly rates is performed using input data from ACLED (Armed Conflict Location and Event Data, https://acleddata.com) and following the method in Prieto-Curiel et al., 2023 (https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0291514). A short description of the method follows:\n1.\tStart with the cumulative total: C(t) = total events (or fatalities) up to week t.\n2.\tFit a piecewise-linear model to this curve.\n3.\tWithin each segment (between change-points), C(t) \u2248 \u03bb \u00d7 t + constant.\n4.\tThe slope \u03bb of each segment is the estimated weekly rate.",
        "dataStructureComponents": {
          "dimensionList": {
            "id": "DimensionDescriptor",
            "dimensions": [
              {
                "id": "REF_AREA",
                "position": 0,
                "type": "Dimension",
                "conceptIdentity": "urn:sdmx:org.sdmx.infomodel.conceptscheme.Concept=OECD.SWAC:CS_SWAC(1.0).REF_AREA",
                "localRepresentation": {
                  "enumeration": "urn:sdmx:org.sdmx.infomodel.codelist.Codelist=OECD:CL_AREA(1.8)"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": "FREQ",
                "position": 1,
                "type": "Dimension",
                "conceptIdentity": "urn:sdmx:org.sdmx.infomodel.conceptscheme.Concept=OECD.SWAC:CS_SWAC(1.0).FREQ",
                "localRepresentation": {
                  "enumeration": "urn:sdmx:org.sdmx.infomodel.codelist.Codelist=SDMX:CL_FREQ(2.1)"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": "MEASURE",
                "position": 2,
                "type": "Dimension",
                "conceptIdentity": "urn:sdmx:org.sdmx.infomodel.conceptscheme.Concept=OECD.SWAC:CS_SWAC(1.0).MEASURE",
                "localRepresentation": {
                  "enumeration": "urn:sdmx:org.sdmx.infomodel.codelist.Codelist=OECD.SWAC:CL_MEASURE_SWAC_PV(1.0)"
                }
              },
